comments.body:
@jamorham there's no way to this without a web browser. This is the de-facto standard for authenticating against a third-party provider (e.g. sign in with Google or Facebook) and there aren't any inherent compatibility or security issues related to the choice of browser. I can give you few examples why it's important for authentication to happen in a web browser - in the near future we'll allow users to enable Multi-Factor authentication, so after they enter their username/password credentials, they'll also be prompted to enter a verification code (could be sent by SMS or obtained from Google authenticator app). Additionally, we might enable sign in/sign up with social providers, so we can't possibly ask all of our partners/clients to implement this functionality. If those flows are fully handled as an interactive browser experience, we can implement them once, and let client only deal with the bear minimum (i.e. implementing the OpenID Connect flow which delegates the authentication and authorization to Tidepool).

To create a client, I'll need to configure the redirect URL. Do you have any preference? Does `xdrip://callback/tidepool` work for you?

@gazhay yes, that's a reference implementation and I suggest you use it for the xDrip integration.

> @jamorham has this been released? If not, I encourage you to do it as soon as possible.

@toddkazakov 
It was released on September 23.
